= Wormwood (Artemisia absinthium)

_Scope: This entry treats_ Artemisia absinthium _L. — grand / common wormwood, the true "wormwood" of European medicine, the bittering principle of_ absinthe _and (historically) of_ vermouth. _The genus_ Artemisia _is large and its species are treated as_ distinct drugs _by every tradition — the entry flags, but does not conflate, the sibling medicinals:_ A. annua _(sweet wormwood, Chinese 青蒿 qīnghāo, the artemisinin/antimalarial plant),_ A. argyi / A. vulgaris _(mugwort, Chinese 艾葉 àiyè, the moxibustion herb),_ A. capillaris / A. scoparia _(Chinese 茵陳 yīnchén, for jaundice),_ A. cina / A. maritima _(Levant wormseed / semen contra, the classical santonin anthelmintic), and_ A. herba-alba / A. judaica _(the desert wormwood_ shīḥ _of the Arabic-speaking world). Status:_ deeply classical in Greco-Roman _(Dioscorides III.23 ἀψίνθιον, Pliny, Galen) and_ well-developed in Tibb _(Avicenna's_ afsantīn_), with significant_ Biblical attestation _(Hebrew_ laʿanah_, Greek_ apsinthos _— the star "Wormwood" of Revelation 8:11)._ Not classical _in Chinese medicine (A. absinthium is the Western_ 洋艾_, not a Bencao drug) or in Ayurveda (the classical_ damanaka _is other Artemisia); those sections are reasoned extension, flagged as such._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Artemisia absinthium_ L., family Asteraceae (Compositae); the drug is the *dried leaves and flowering tops* (_Absinthii herba_). The genus name _Artemisia_ is ancient and doubly attributed: to the goddess *Ἄρτεμις (Artemis)* — patroness of the wild and of women's medicine — and, per Pliny, to *Artemisia II of Caria* (d. 350 BCE), the botanist-queen of Halicarnassus and widow of Mausolus, said to have given her name to the plant. The species epithet _absinthium_ is a direct Latinization of Greek *ἀψίνθιον (apsínthion)*, a word of *uncertain, probably non-Greek (pre-Greek or Near-Eastern loan) origin* — the ancient folk connection to _a-_ (privative) + _psinthos_ ("pleasure," i.e. "undelightful," for the bitterness) is *folk etymology*.#footnote[Genus etymology and the Artemis / Artemisia-of-Caria double attribution: Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XXV.36; Lewis & Short (s.v. _Artemisia_, _absinthium_). Greek _ἀψίνθιον_ as a word of unknown, likely pre-Greek/loan origin: Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. ἀψίνθιον); Chantraine, _Dictionnaire étymologique de la langue grecque_ — both reject the _a-privative + psinthos_ "no-pleasure" derivation as folk etymology. The plant is _the_ proverbially bitter herb of antiquity.]

*English:* _Wormwood_ — *not* transparently "worm" + "wood." The word is Old English *wermōd* (cf. Old High German _wermuota_, modern German *Wermut*), of *obscure origin*; Middle English reshaped _wermōd_ toward _worm-wood_ by *folk etymology*, assisted by the herb's genuine anthelmintic ("worm-expelling") use. The "wood" element likewise has nothing to do with timber. Crucially, German *Wermut* is the direct source of English *vermouth* (via French _vermout_): the aromatized fortified wine is named for wormwood, its original defining botanical — so "vermouth" _is_ etymologically "wormwood."#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "wormwood, n." and "vermouth, n."): OE _wermōd_ of unknown origin, remodelled to _worm-wood_ by folk etymology; "vermouth" < French _vermout_ < German _Wermut_ = wormwood. The "worm" and "wood" elements are both secondary folk-etymological reshapings, flagged as such.]

*Chinese:* _A. absinthium_ is *not a classical Bencao drug* — it is the Western species. Modern Chinese: *苦艾 (kǔ'ài)*, "bitter mugwort," and *洋艾 (yáng'ài)*, "ocean/foreign mugwort" (洋 = "of overseas," the standard modern prefix for Western introductions). The *classically* canonical _Artemisia_ drugs are different species and must not be confused with it: *艾葉 (àiyè)*, mugwort leaf (_A. argyi_), the moxibustion herb; *青蒿 (qīnghāo)*, sweet wormwood (_A. annua_), the summerheat-clearing and antimalarial drug that yielded artemisinin; and *茵陳(蒿) (yīnchén(hāo))* (_A. capillaris / A. scoparia_), the premier drug for damp-heat jaundice.#footnote[苦艾 / 洋艾 as the modern Chinese names for _A. absinthium_: standard modern Chinese phytotherapy and botanical-translation usage; there is no 本草綱目 entry under a classical name for this Western species. The classical Artemisia drugs 艾葉, 青蒿, 茵陳 (distinct species) per Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004).]

*Sanskrit:* _A. absinthium_ proper is *not classical in Ayurveda*. The classical Sanskrit *दमनक (damanaka)* and *नागदमनी (nāgadamanī)* denote aromatic _Artemisia_ species (commonly identified with _A. pallens_, _A. nilagirica_, or _A. vulgaris_) — valued as much for fragrance and ritual (the _damanakāropaṇa_ spring worship of Śiva/Kāma with damanaka sprigs) as for medicine. _A. absinthium_ entered Indian usage post-classically, largely through Unani channels, under the borrowed name *अफ़संतीन (afsantīn)* / Hindi *विलायती अफ़संतीन (vilāyatī afsantīn)*, "foreign afsantīn."#footnote[_Damanaka_ / _nāgadamanī_ as aromatic-and-ritual Artemisia (not _A. absinthium_): Bhāvaprakāśa Nighaṇṭu, _Puṣpavarga / Karpūrādi-varga_; P. V. Sharma, _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_, vol. II; the _damanakāropaṇa_ festival is a well-attested classical spring rite. The Sanskrit species identification of "damanaka" is commentarial and not uniform. _A. absinthium_ under the borrowed _afsantīn_ is a post-classical Unani import into Indian usage — flagged.]

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *أفسنتين (afsantīn)* — a direct loan from Greek _apsínthion_ (sometimes *أفسنتين رومي, afsantīn rūmī*, "Roman/Byzantine wormwood," marking its Greek pedigree). This is distinct from the native desert Artemisia *شيح (shīḥ)* (_A. herba-alba_, _A. judaica_), the Levantine-and-North-African steppe wormwood, and from *درمنة (dirmana)* / *شيح خراساني*, the Khorasani wormseed (_A. cina_) that yields santonin. Persian: *أفسنتین (afsantīn)*; Urdu/Hakeemi: *افسنتین (afsantīn)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Afsantīn_ (< Gk. _apsinthion_) for _A. absinthium_ in the Greco-Arabic tradition, distinguished from native _shīḥ_ (_A. herba-alba_/_judaica_) and the santonin-bearing _dirmana_/_shīḥ khurāsānī_ (_A. cina_): Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn fī al-Ṭibb_, Book II (_afsantīn_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_ (_afsantīn_, _shīḥ_ entries). The three are treated as related but distinct simples.]

*Greek & Hebrew (Biblical, treated below):* Greek *ἄψινθος (ápsinthos)* and Hebrew *לַעֲנָה (laʿănāh)* — the biblical "wormwood," the standing metaphor for bitterness and poison; see the Biblical subsection under Classical Formulas.

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Artemisia absinthium has no classical Chinese entry_ — it is the Western 洋艾, outside the Bencao tradition. The reading below is *reasoned extension* on classical axes, *not* canonical 本草, and is anchored partly by analogy to the genuinely classical Artemisia drugs (青蒿, 艾葉, 茵陳). Flagged.

*Property (by reasoned extension):* Cool-to-neutral in its bitter-draining aspect, with a warm-aromatic note from the essential oil — an unusual split; the intense bitterness dominates. Provisionally *neutral (平) tending cool (涼)*.

*Flavor:* Bitter (苦 kǔ) — extremely; pungent-aromatic (辛芳香) secondarily.

*Channels (by extension):* Liver (肝), Gallbladder (膽), Spleen (脾), Stomach (胃) — the placement for a bitter hepato-digestive drying drug.

*Actions (by extension):*
- *疏肝利膽 (shū gān lì dǎn)* — courses the Liver and benefits the Gallbladder; the bitter hepatic-choleretic register (by analogy to 茵陳 for damp-heat).
- *健脾消食 / 開胃 (jiàn pí xiāo shí / kāi wèi)* — the bitter-stomachic, appetite-opening action — wormwood's single best-attested cross-system use.
- *清熱燥濕 (qīng rè zào shī)* — clears heat and dries damp (the 苦寒燥濕 logic of a strong bitter).
- *殺蟲 (shā chóng)* — kills worms/parasites; the traditional anthelmintic use (shared, in the genus, with 青蒿's antimalarial 截瘧 and the santonin of _A. cina_).
- *退黃 (tuì huáng)* — abates jaundice (by extension from 茵陳).

*Cautions (by extension):* Spleen/Stomach cold-vacuity (the bitterness injures the middle); pregnancy (emmenagogue); the essential-oil/thujone toxicity places a hard ceiling on dose that classical bitter-drug practice would express as "do not use long, do not overdose."

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical, for_ Artemisia absinthium. _The classical_ damanaka _is a different, aromatic Artemisia; A. absinthium is a post-classical Unani import (afsantīn). The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only entries._

*Rasa (taste):* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter) — predominant and extreme; Kaṭu (कटु, pungent) secondary.

*Guṇa (qualities):* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp/penetrating).

*Vīrya (potency):* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) — converging with the Galenic and Tibb "hot" reading, and diverging from the cool reading a pure-bitter drug would otherwise suggest (the aromatic oil carries the heat).

*Vipāka (post-digestive taste):* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva (specific action):* No prabhāva can be assigned classically (no classical entry). If pressed, the *anthelmintic (krimighna) potency out of proportion to a merely bitter-pungent-hot drug*, and the near-superlative bitterness (absinthin) that makes it a reference _tikta_ substance, are the candidate prabhāva-level features — flagged as modern interpretive.

*Doṣa effect:* Kapha-śāmaka (bitter-pungent-dry-hot clears Kapha strongly) and Pitta-śāmaka in its bitter aspect, though the uṣṇa vīrya tempers the Pitta-pacification; Vāta-vardhaka in excess (the rūkṣa-tikta profile aggravates Vāta) — a standard tension of hot bitters.

*Karma (by extension):*
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — the bitter-stomachic kindling of _agni_ and digestion of _āma_ (the headline action).
- _Krimighna_ — anthelmintic (the traditional and etymological core use).
- _Yakṛt-uttejaka, pittasāraka_ — hepatic stimulant / choleretic (interpretive).
- _Jvarahara_ — antipyretic (by genus analogy to _A. annua_'s antimalarial action — interpretive, not a claim that _absinthium_ is antimalarial).
- _Ārtavajanana_ — emmenagogue (with the pregnancy caution that entails).
- _Kaphahara, vāta-anulomana_ (modest).

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha entries for A. absinthium — extensions of conditional rules; tags: S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(S, extended) Conditional:* Vāta excess and _rūkṣa_ states (the dry-sharp-bitter profile aggravates Vāta); high Pitta only insofar as the uṣṇa vīrya bites.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Pregnancy and lactation* — *contraindicated*: emmenagogue/abortifacient tradition plus thujone neurotoxicity.
  - *Epilepsy / seizure disorders* — *contraindicated*: thujone is a GABA-A antagonist and convulsant.
  - *Prolonged / high-dose use* — avoid: cumulative thujone toxicity ("absinthism": the historical syndrome, though modern analysis attributes most of it to alcohol — see pharmacology).
  - *Asteraceae allergy* — cross-reactivity (ragweed, chamomile, chrysanthemum).
  - *Gastric/duodenal ulcer, hyperacidity* — the intense bitter secretagogue effect may aggravate.

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Wormwood is *fully classical in Tibb* under the Greek-loan name *أفسنتين (afsantīn)* — one of the tradition's principal *stomach-and-liver tonics and deobstruents*, inherited directly from Dioscorides and Galen and elaborated by Avicenna and Ibn al-Bayṭār. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ` for the framework.

*Arabic:* *أفسنتين (afsantīn)* (also *أفسنتين رومي, afsantīn rūmī*); the native desert wormwood *شيح (shīḥ)* (_A. herba-alba/judaica_) and the santonin wormseed *درمنة / شيح خراساني (dirmana / shīḥ khurāsānī)* (_A. cina_) are treated as related simples. Persian: *أفسنتین (afsantīn)*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *افسنتین (afsantīn)*.

*Mizāj:* *Hot in the 1st degree, Dry in the 2nd–3rd degree* per Avicenna (sources vary on the exact degree of dryness) — a warm, strongly drying bitter. Converges with Galen's "hot and dry."#footnote[Mizāj and actions of _afsantīn_: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_afsantīn_); the precise darajāt for dryness vary across manuscript and later Unani sources (2nd vs 3rd degree) — flagged for verification against a specific edition.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī al-maʿida* — stomach tonic (the signature action); *mushahhī* — appetizer; *qābiḍ* — astringent-toning to the stomach mouth.
- *Muqawwī al-kabid* and *mufattiḥ li-suddad al-kabid wa-l-ṭiḥāl* — liver tonic and deobstruent of hepatic and splenic obstruction.
- *Muḵrij / qātil li-l-dīdān* — expels/kills intestinal worms (the anthelmintic core).
- *Mudirr li-l-bawl wa-l-ḥayḍ* — diuretic and emmenagogue.
- *Dāfiʿ li-l-ḥummā* — antipyretic, for intermittent (marsh) fevers.
- *Ḥāfiẓ* — a preservative: afsantīn laid among clothes and books protects them from moth and decay (the same property that made "wormwood" the moth-herb of Europe).
- *Muḥallil*, *jālī* (resolvent, cleansing); a component of *tiryāqī* (antidotal) preparations.

*Istemālāt (indications):* weak digestion and anorexia; hepatic and splenic obstruction and _yaraqān_ (jaundice); intestinal worms; intermittent fevers; menstrual retention; _istisqāʾ_ (dropsy, via the hepatic-diuretic action).

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* hot/_ṣafrāwī_ (bilious) constitutions and hot-pattern conditions; pregnancy (emmenagogue); the drug provokes _ṣudāʿ_ (headache) in hot temperaments and, in excess, nausea/vomiting; its bitterness is poorly tolerated.

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* aniseed (_anīsūn_), mastic (_maṣṭakā_), gum tragacanth (_kathīrāʾ_), and honey — to blunt the bitterness and the drying-heating edge.

*Badal (substitutes):* *shīḥ* (_A. herba-alba_) and *qayṣūm* (_A. abrotanum_, southernwood) for the aromatic-anthelmintic action; *ghāfith* (agrimony) for the hepatic-deobstruent action.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* _A. absinthium_ (afsantīn) is a *Greco-Arabic (Dioscoridean) drug, not a Prophetic-medicine substance* — it has no Qurʾānic or sound-hadith attestation, and I flag it as such rather than manufacturing one. The native *shīḥ* (desert Artemisia) carries some folk-Prophetic and Bedouin-medical register, but that is a different plant.#footnote[Tibb sources: Ibn Sīnā, _al-Qānūn_ II (_afsantīn_); Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ li-Mufradāt al-Adwiya_ (_afsantīn_, _shīḥ_); al-Rāzī, _al-Ḥāwī_; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_. The Greco-Arabic (rather than Nabawī) provenance of _afsantīn_ is stated honestly; no hadith is asserted for it.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formula uses _A. absinthium_ (the Western species). The genuinely classical Artemisia formulas belong to *other species* and are noted only for contrast: *艾葉* (mugwort, _A. argyi_) in moxibustion (灸) and in 膠艾湯 (Jiāo Ài Tāng) for uterine bleeding; *青蒿* (_A. annua_) in 蒿芩清膽湯 (Hāo Qín Qīng Dǎn Tāng) and, most famously, the antimalarial use recorded by Ge Hong (葛洪, _Zhǒuhòu Bèijí Fāng_ 肘後備急方, 4th c.) that guided the isolation of artemisinin; *茵陳* (_A. capillaris_) in 茵陳蒿湯 (Yīnchénhāo Tāng) for damp-heat jaundice. Modern Chinese use of 苦艾/洋艾 is post-classical and Western-derived.#footnote[Classical Artemisia formulas (distinct species): Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009); the Ge Hong _Zhǒuhòu Bèijí Fāng_ 青蒿 note is the source famously cited by Tu Youyou (屠呦呦) in the artemisinin work (2015 Nobel). _A. absinthium_ is not among these.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Sanskrit formula uses _A. absinthium_. _Damanaka_ (aromatic Artemisia) appears in fragrance, ritual (_damanakāropaṇa_), and minor aromatic-carminative use; _A. absinthium_ proper enters Indian practice only through Unani _afsantīn_ preparations (below). Post-classical, flagged.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*The deepest and most continuous tradition for this herb.*

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ III.23, _apsínthion_) treats wormwood as a leading stomachic, anthelmintic, and hepatic/renal drug, and describes *ἀψινθίτης οἶνος (apsinthítēs oînos)*, "wine of wormwood," for the stomach, appetite, and jaundice — the direct ancestor of vermouth. *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XXVII) gives extensive uses and records the Roman custom by which the victor of the chariot races at the _Feriae Latinae_ drank wormwood wine — a ritual health-draught, "because they thought health a fit reward." *Galen* classes it hot-and-dry and makes it the type-specimen of the bitter stomachic (_amarum_).
- *Galenic / official preparations:* wormwood was long officinal — *Absinthii herba*, *Tinctura Absinthii*, *Extractum Absinthii*, *Vinum Absinthites* — the archetypal *amarum* (simple bitter tonic) taken before meals for appetite and digestion. Culpeper distinguishes common, Roman (_A. pontica_), and sea wormwoods. The *moth-repellent* use (sprigs in the wardrobe; French _garde-robe_) parallels the Tibb _ḥāfiẓ_ property.
- *Absinthe:* the distilled spirit flavored with wormwood, green anise, and Florence fennel, developed in late-18th-c. Switzerland (Val-de-Travers; the Henriod sisters / Dr. Pierre Ordinaire, ~1792) and commercialized by *Pernod* (Pontarlier, from 1805). "La Fée Verte" ("the Green Fairy") of the 19th-c. artistic demimonde; blamed for *absinthism* and *banned* across much of Europe and the US ~1908–1915. Modern chemical analysis of pre-ban absinthe found *thujone levels far lower than the myth held*, indicting the alcohol (and adulterants) rather than thujone for most of the harm; absinthe is again legal under regulated thujone limits.
- *Vermouth and amari:* *vermouth* — aromatized fortified wine, named for wormwood (German _Wermut_) — was commercialized in Turin by *Antonio Benedetto Carpano* (1786); wormwood was the defining botanical (now often reduced or replaced). Wormwood also flavors many Italian *amari* and traditional bitters — the continuous descent of Dioscorides' _apsinthites oinos_ into the modern aperitivo-digestivo.#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.23 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XXVII (Loeb/Jones) — the _Feriae Latinae_ wormwood-wine custom; Galen, _De simplicium medicamentorum facultatibus_. Officinal preparations and the three wormwoods: Culpeper, _The English Physician_; standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_). Absinthe history and the thujone re-analysis: Lachenmeier et al., "Chemical composition of vintage preban absinthe," _J. Agric. Food Chem._ (2008) and related work — verify current literature; the alcohol-not-thujone reassessment is now the mainstream view. Vermouth/Carpano and the amaro tradition: standard drinks-history references. The German _Wermut_ → vermouth etymology: OED.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above. The canonical preparations: *Sharāb al-afsantīn* (wormwood wine/syrup — the Greco-Arabic continuation of Dioscorides' _apsinthites oinos_, as a stomachic and hepatic deobstruent), *Sharbat-i afsantīn*, and afsantīn as a bitter-stomachic-and-deobstruent component of compound *maʿjūn* electuaries and hepatic formulas in Unani practice (e.g. the _Maʿjūn_-class stomachics and liver correctives of the _National Formulary of Unani Medicine_).

=== Biblical (laʿănāh / apsinthos)

Wormwood is one of Scripture's great *metaphors of bitterness and divine judgment* — its medical register is secondary to its symbolic one. Hebrew *לַעֲנָה (laʿănāh)*, "wormwood," is paired throughout the Hebrew Bible with *רֹאשׁ (rōʾš)*, "gall/poison," and with bitterness:
- *Deuteronomy 29:18 (29:17 MT)* — "a root that beareth gall (_rōʾš_) and wormwood (_laʿănāh_)," the idolater turning from the covenant.
- *Proverbs 5:4* — the end of the strange woman is "bitter as wormwood."
- *Jeremiah 9:15; 23:15* — God feeds the faithless people "wormwood" and "water of gall."
- *Lamentations 3:15, 19* — "He hath filled me with bitterness, he hath made me drunken with wormwood."
- *Amos 5:7; 6:12* — those who "turn judgment to wormwood," i.e. pervert justice into bitterness.
In the New Testament, Greek *ἄψινθος (ápsinthos)* names the apocalyptic star of *Revelation 8:11*: "and the name of the star is called Wormwood: and the third part of the waters became wormwood; and many men died of the waters, because they were made bitter." The biblical _laʿănāh_ is most likely one of the *Levantine desert Artemisia* species (_A. herba-alba_, _A. judaica_ — the Arabic _shīḥ_) rather than _A. absinthium_ specifically; the identification is by bitterness and habitat, and I flag it as such.#footnote[Biblical wormwood: Hebrew _laʿănāh_ (Deut. 29:18; Prov. 5:4; Jer. 9:15, 23:15; Lam. 3:15, 19; Amos 5:7, 6:12) and Greek _ápsinthos_ (Rev. 8:11). Botanical identification of biblical wormwood with Levantine _Artemisia_ (esp. _A. herba-alba_/_A. judaica_) rather than European _A. absinthium_: Zohary, _Plants of the Bible_ (Cambridge, 1982). The species identification is by ecology and the bitterness-metaphor, not botanically explicit in the text — flagged.]

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_A. absinthium_ is a *temperate Eurasian* plant — native across Europe, North Africa, and temperate Asia, and widely naturalized in temperate North and South America — of dry, stony, sunny waste ground. Its medicinal intensity (bitter sesquiterpene lactones + thujone-bearing oil) is a *dry-slope, full-sun adaptation*, densest in warm-dry summer conditions. See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-dry to continental-temperate Europe and the Mediterranean fringe (the canonical home)

Central and southern European hills, the Alpine foothills, the Mediterranean dry margins, and the temperate steppe. In _ṛtucaryā_ terms the relevant load is _grīṣma_–_śarad_ Pitta with autumn dryness; in 中醫 terms 燥 (dryness) with summer 暑 on the Liver–Stomach; in Tibb terms a Hot-Dry (_ṣafrāwī_) summer pressure.

*Traditional preparation.* The *pre-meal bitter* is the constitutive form: _apsinthites oinos_ → _vinum absinthites_ → *vermouth and the amari*, taken in small measure as an *aperitivo/digestivo* to kindle appetite and digestion before or after a rich meal. Wormwood *tea/tincture* as the household bitter tonic and vermifuge. The Alpine-and-continental European aperitif-and-digestif culture (vermouth, absinthe, amaro) is the living descent of the classical stomachic-wine.

*Cares.* The bitter-hot-dry drug is climatically apt as a *small-dose corrective before/after heavy meals* in the temperate cool-season register, not as a bulk food; its correctives (anise, honey, sweet fortified wine) blunt the drying-heating edge — the vermouth formula (wormwood + sweetening + fortified wine + aromatics) is itself a constitutional _muṣliḥ_ construction, exactly parallel to the Tibb pairing of afsantīn with aniseed and honey.

=== Climates where wormwood is not traditional

Hot-humid tropical, cold-dry high-altitude, and cold-damp Atlantic zones have no indigenous _A. absinthium_ tradition; its spread there is post-classical (colonial and commercial, via absinthe/vermouth). The bitter-digestive _indication_ travels, but the plant's constitutional home is temperate-continental dry ground.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

The globalized vermouth-and-absinthe trade carries wormwood into every climate as a *recreational* rather than *medicinal* form — and there the classical logic inverts: the traditional register was a *small pre-/post-prandial bitter corrective*, whereas the modern register is *alcohol-forward volume consumption*, in which the herb's benefit is lost and the alcohol harm dominates (the real lesson of the absinthe episode). Taking wormwood as a large-volume intoxicant rather than a thimble-measure bitter is a _mātrā-viruddha_ (wrong-quantity) as much as a _deśa-viruddha_ error; the seasonal point — bitters suit the heavy-meal cool season more than high summer in hot constitutions — is the _kāla-viruddha_ parallel. See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Wormwood is a *medicinal/flavoring herb, not a food*; the pharmacology carries a genuine neurotoxic safety signal (thujone).

*Key constituents:*
- *Bitter sesquiterpene lactones* — *absinthin* and *anabsinthin* (dimeric guaianolides), among the *bitterest known natural compounds* (bitterness threshold in the parts-per-ten-million range); plus artabsin, matricin. These are the basis of the bitter-tonic/secretagogue action and of the _tikta_/苦/_amarum_ classification across all systems.
- *Essential oil (0.2–1.5%)* — dominated (chemotype-dependent) by *α- and β-thujone* (monoterpene ketones; the toxic principle), with *thujyl alcohol and acetate*, *(Z)-epoxyocimene*, *chamazulene* (the blue anti-inflammatory azulene formed on distillation), *sabinene*, *myrcene*, *linalool*, others. Thujone content varies widely by chemotype and provenance.
- *Flavonoids and phenolic acids* — quercetin and rutin glycosides, chlorogenic and caffeic acids (antioxidant fraction).
- *Note on what wormwood is* not: *artemisinin* is from _A. annua_ (青蒿), *not* _A. absinthium_ — grand wormwood is *not* an antimalarial source; and *santonin*, the classical isolated anthelmintic, is from _A. cina_ (wormseed), not from _absinthium_ (whose anthelmintic use is by the whole herb/oil). Both confusions are common and are flagged here.#footnote[Phytochemistry: absinthin/anabsinthin as reference-bitter guaianolide dimers, and the α-/β-thujone-dominated oil with chamazulene: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); ESCOP and EMA/HMPC assessments of _Absinthii herba_. The artemisinin (_A. annua_) and santonin (_A. cina_) distinctions from _A. absinthium_ are taxonomically and pharmacologically important and frequently confused in popular sources.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Bitter secretagogue / digestive tonic — the headline, cross-system-validated action.* The intensely bitter sesquiterpene lactones stimulate, via bitter (T2R) receptors, salivary, gastric, and biliary secretion and vagal tone — the mechanistic correlate of the Greco-Roman _amarum_, the Tibb _muqawwī al-maʿida_, and the reasoned _dīpana-pācana_/健脾開胃 readings. Best-supported use.
- *Thujone neurotoxicity — the central safety issue.* α-Thujone is a *GABA-A receptor antagonist* (blocks the chloride channel), hence a *convulsant/proconvulsant* and the basis of the historical "absinthism" picture (though modern reassessment attributes most 19th-c. absinthe harm to alcoholism and adulteration, not thujone). This grounds the epilepsy and prolonged-use contraindications.
- *Anthelmintic / antiparasitic* — traditional and preclinical; the etymological "worm-wood" core; whole-herb, not a purified drug.
- *Anti-inflammatory / IBD signal* — a small German double-blind RCT of a wormwood preparation in Crohn's disease reported a *steroid-sparing* benefit (Omer et al., 2007); preliminary and needing replication. Chamazulene and flavonoids contribute anti-inflammatory/antioxidant activity.
- *Antimicrobial, hepatoprotective, appetite-stimulant* — preclinical support, modest clinical translation.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* the limiting compound is *thujone*, not herb weight.
- *Bitter tonic (traditional):* ~*1–1.5 g dried herb* as an infusion, or *1–3 mL of tincture*, shortly before meals; *short courses only* (traditionally 2–4 weeks, not continuous).
- *Thujone regulatory ceiling:* the EU limits thujone in *alcoholic beverages* (broadly *~35 mg/kg* in bitters/vermouth-class, *~10 mg/kg* in most others; figures revised over time — verify current EU Regulation) and restricts thujone from added flavorings in food. These beverage limits are the practical reference; isolated-thujone intake should be minimized.
- *Contraindicated:* *pregnancy and lactation* (emmenagogue/abortifacient + neurotoxin); *epilepsy/seizure disorders* (convulsant); *A. absinthium* essential oil should *not* be taken neat.
- *Cautions:* Asteraceae allergy; peptic ulcer/hyperacidity; hepatic/renal disease at high dose; do not use long-term or in high dose.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice* — thujone toxicity is real and dose-dependent; therapeutic or high-dose use warrants clinician supervision.#footnote[Dose ranges: ESCOP _Monographs_ and EMA/HMPC assessment report on _Artemisia absinthium_ herba; thujone limits: EU Regulation on flavourings and food ingredients with flavouring properties (the α-/β-thujone maxima for beverage categories have been revised — verify the current figures). The Crohn's RCT: Omer et al., "Steroid-sparing effect of wormwood (_Artemisia absinthium_) in Crohn's disease," _Phytomedicine_ 14 (2007) — small, preliminary. These are reference thresholds requiring current verification, not medical advice; pregnancy and epilepsy are firm contraindications.]

